.\"
.\" KSEM_OPEN(2) - Substrate Native Personality
.\"
.TH KSEM_OPEN 2 "2026-10-19" "Substrate OS" "System Calls"
.SH NAME
ksem_open, ksem_init, ksem_close, ksem_unlink, ksem_wait, ksem_trywait,
ksem_timedwait, ksem_post, ksem_getvalue \- kernel-backed POSIX semaphore
objects
.SH LIBRARY
Standard C library
.RI ( libc ", " \-lc )
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <sys/syscall.h>
.B #include <semaphore.h>
.PP
.BI "int syscall(SYS_KSEM_OPEN, const char *" name ", int " oflag ,
.BI "            mode_t " mode ", unsigned int " value ");"
.BI "int syscall(SYS_KSEM_INIT, const sem_t *" sem ", unsigned int " value ");"
.BI "int syscall(SYS_KSEM_CLOSE, int " id ");"
.BI "int syscall(SYS_KSEM_UNLINK, const char *" name ");"
.BI "int syscall(SYS_KSEM_WAIT, int " id ", const sem_t *" sem ");"
.BI "int syscall(SYS_KSEM_TRYWAIT, int " id ", const sem_t *" sem ");"
.BI "int syscall(SYS_KSEM_TIMEDWAIT, int " id ", const struct timespec *" abstime ,
.BI "            const sem_t *" sem ");"
.BI "int syscall(SYS_KSEM_POST, int " id ", const sem_t *" sem ");"
.BI "int syscall(SYS_KSEM_GETVALUE, int " id ", int *" sval ", const sem_t *" sem ");"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B ksem
system calls implement kernel-backed POSIX counting semaphores.  They are the
Substrate-native primitives beneath the
.BR sem_open (3)
family and beneath process-shared
.BR sem_init (3)
(pshared != 0).  A semaphore lives as a system-wide kernel object rather than a
word in user memory, so it synchronises correctly across unrelated processes \(em
Substrate's futex is keyed by a per-process virtual address and never crosses an
address space, so a genuinely shared semaphore has to live in the kernel.  The
model mirrors FreeBSD's
.IR ksem .
.PP
.BR ksem_open ()
creates or attaches to a semaphore and returns a non-negative
.I id
(a descriptor) used by every other call.  If
.I name
is
.B NULL
an anonymous, unnamed object is created; otherwise
.I name
must begin with a slash
.RB ( / )
followed by one or more characters.
.I oflag
may contain
.B O_CREAT
(create if absent, using
.I mode
and
.IR value )
and
.B O_EXCL
(with
.BR O_CREAT ,
fail if the name already exists).  Each open adds one reference.
.PP
.BR ksem_init ()
creates the anonymous semaphore behind a process-shared
.BR sem_init (3),
with initial
.IR value ,
and returns its
.IR id .
It is bound to the memory
.I sem
lives in: any process that maps that same memory (a
.B MAP_SHARED
mapping of the same file or shared object, an inherited shared anonymous
mapping, or the same System V shared-memory segment) may use it by passing
its own address of that
.BR sem_t ,
whatever address it is mapped at.  The semaphore has no permission bits
(mode 0), so a process that does not map the memory, does not hold the
semaphore open, and is not the superuser cannot use it, even knowing its
.IR id .
The creating process holds one reference.
.PP
.BR ksem_close ()
drops one reference held by the calling process.  When the last reference is
dropped and the object has been unlinked (or is anonymous), it is destroyed.
.PP
.BR ksem_unlink ()
removes
.I name
so later opens miss it; existing descriptors keep working until closed.
.PP
.BR ksem_wait ()
decrements the value, blocking interruptibly while it is zero.
.BR ksem_trywait ()
is the non-blocking form.
.BR ksem_timedwait ()
blocks only until the absolute
.B CLOCK_REALTIME
deadline
.IR abstime .
.BR ksem_post ()
increments the value and wakes one waiter.
.BR ksem_getvalue ()
stores the current value in
.IR *sval .
.PP
The wait calls and
.BR ksem_post ()
need the caller to hold the semaphore open, to map the memory a
.BR ksem_init ()
semaphore is bound to (the
.I sem
argument), or to have write permission on it;
.BR ksem_getvalue ()
needs one of the first two or read permission.
.I sem
may be
.B NULL
when the caller has no
.BR sem_t ;
the other two ways in still apply.
.BR fork (2)
gives the child a reference to each semaphore the parent holds open.
A process's still-open descriptors are dropped automatically when it exits.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR ksem_open ()
and
.BR ksem_init ()
return a non-negative descriptor.  The others return 0.  All return a negative
errno value on failure (the C library negates it into
.I errno
and returns \-1).
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EACCES
Permission to open or unlink the semaphore is denied.
.TP
.B EAGAIN
.RB ( ksem_trywait ())
The value is zero.
.TP
.B EEXIST
.B O_CREAT
and
.B O_EXCL
were set but the name already exists.
.TP
.B EINVAL
Bad
.IR id ,
or one the caller neither holds open, nor maps the memory of, nor has
permission to use;
.I name
does not begin with a slash and a character; or
.I value
exceeds the maximum.
.TP
.B EFAULT
.RB ( ksem_init ())
.I sem
is not a mapped address.
.TP
.B EINTR
A blocking wait was interrupted by a signal.
.TP
.B ENAMETOOLONG
.I name
is too long.
.TP
.B ENOENT
.B O_CREAT
was not set and the name does not exist.
.TP
.B ENOSPC
The system-wide limit on ksem objects would be exceeded.
.TP
.B ERANGE
.RB ( ksem_post ())
The increment would raise the value past
.BR SEM_VALUE_MAX .
.TP
.B ETIMEDOUT
.RB ( ksem_timedwait ())
The deadline elapsed before the semaphore could be locked.
.SH NOTES
These calls are Substrate-specific and are normally reached through the POSIX
.BR sem_open (3)
wrappers rather than
.BR syscall (2)
directly.
.SH EXAMPLE
.RS 4
.EX
#include <sys/syscall.h>
#include <fcntl.h>

int id = syscall(SYS_KSEM_OPEN, "/demo", O_CREAT | O_RDWR, 0666, 0);
if (id < 0) { /* -id is the errno */ }
syscall(SYS_KSEM_POST, id, NULL);
syscall(SYS_KSEM_WAIT, id, NULL);
syscall(SYS_KSEM_CLOSE, id);
syscall(SYS_KSEM_UNLINK, "/demo");
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sem_open (3),
.BR sem_close (3),
.BR sem_unlink (3),
.BR sem_wait (3),
.BR sem_post (3),
.BR sem_getvalue (3),
.BR semop (2)
